WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.121 --> 00:00:01.050
(intriguing music)

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.460
- [Narrator] House Speaker Kevin McCarthy

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.230
can't seem to catch a break.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.370
- [Reporter] 15 rounds of voting

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.010
before McCarthy was able to
take control of the gavel.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.140
- Political battle over
raising the debt ceiling.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.490
- [Reporter] Chaos in Congress

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.430
as the government lurches
towards a possible shutdown.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.470
- [Narrator] McCarthy
has a razor-thin majority

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.090
in the GOP-led House

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.790
and is facing a looming
partial government shutdown

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.620
that could cost him the gavel.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.480
- Many Republicans think
he's doing the best

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.313
that anyone could do,

00:00:25.313 --> 00:00:27.630
but you have a number of
very hard-line Republicans

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.890
who either want him out

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.840
or want him to do exactly what they say,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.990
and it's gonna be really hard
to satisfy both of those wings

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.760
with such a small majority.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.260
- [Narrator] Here's a
look at the power struggle

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.760
amongst House Republicans

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.940
that is affecting McCarthy's
control over his own party

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.463
and how that could lead
to a government shutdown.

00:00:47.152 --> 00:00:47.985
(gentle music)

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:50.040
House lawmakers are scrambling to pass

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.740
a short-term spending
bill by September 30th,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.030
the end of the fiscal year,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.370
to avoid a partial government shutdown.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.660
The only problem,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.600
the majority of House
Republicans can't seem to agree

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.430
on what goes in that bill.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.750
A faction of Republicans

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.970
is threatening not to pass legislation

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.130
if McCarthy doesn't include their demands

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.020
in any funding deal.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.250
- It's a group of Republicans

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.690
who are willing to basically

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.850
undercut the party's
legislation to make a point

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.040
and to try to, you know,
hold out for a better deal.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.180
- [Narrator] This group of lawmakers

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.520
all agree on the need to do one thing:

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.230
cut government spending.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.190
But some in the group

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.560
are also making other disparate demands,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.833
like ending aid to Ukraine and
tightening border security.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.950
Some are also threatening to oust McCarthy

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.050
if he doesn't meet their demands.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.340
- Members of the Freedom Caucus

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.110
are very, you know, hard line,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.240
the members who want to sharply
cut government spending.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.010
But then there's other
folks like Matt Gaetz

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.420
who's not a member of the Freedom Caucus

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.150
but is a very, very
outspoken critic of McCarthy.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.770
He's got a whole list of demands

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.000
and things he wants McCarthy to do,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.560
but at the end of the day,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.190
he really just doesn't
want McCarthy as speaker.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.650
- The path forward for the
House of Representatives

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.650
is to either bring you into
immediate total compliance

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.863
or remove you pursuant to a
motion to vacate the chair.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.050
- [Narrator] This isn't the first time

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.000
McCarthy has gone head to head with Gaetz

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.280
and other hard-line House Republicans.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.560
- A speaker has not been elected.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.690
A speaker has not been elected.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.420
A speaker has not been elected.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.410
- [Narrator] Back in January,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.170
McCarthy struggled to obtain
the majority of votes needed

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.370
to become speaker,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.398
enduring 15 rounds of voting
before he won the seat.

00:02:26.398 --> 00:02:29.400
(lawmakers applauding and cheering)

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.730
Gaetz and several other
Republicans led that opposition,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.850
and to win them over, McCarthy
made several concessions,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.370
like promising deep spending cuts.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.150
- The problem is that every
time he gets one thing done,

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.120
he makes promises to some
members about doing future things

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.280
to help them out or to win them over,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.073
and at some point all those
promises start to come due.

00:02:52.128 --> 00:02:53.010
(lawmakers applauding)

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.920
- [Narrator] McCarthy's plight
is an unfamiliar territory.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.360
- John Boehner.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.120
- [Narrator] Speaker John
Boehner resigned in 2015

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.370
after being squeezed by clashing factions

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.600
within his own party

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.780
over spending cuts needed to
avoid a government shutdown.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.000
- Republican House Speakers
have a long history

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.370
of having a lot of dissidents
who nip at their heels

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.330
for years and years

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:13.950
and try to push them on issues including,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.960
you know, deficits, debts, and spending.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.570
What is new is that his
majority is so small

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.370
that he cannot just ignore people.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.660
He has to actually deal with them

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.310
in order to get legislation passed.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.640
Past speakers could probably
ignore 10 or 15 or 20 members

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.890
and still get bills over the finish line,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:29.823
McCarthy cannot.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.850
- [Narrator] To pass the
short-term spending bill,

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.820
McCarthy can't afford to
lose more than four votes

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.773
without help from Democrats.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.340
- Let's make sure the
government stays open

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.960
while we finish doing our job,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.573
passing all the individual
bills and getting this stuff.

00:03:45.573 --> 00:03:47.910
- McCarthy's, you know, is
a very personable leader.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.260
He likes having relationships.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.570
He's not so much a policy guy,

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:53.460
so it really is a matter
of managing his caucus,

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.200
getting a bill they can pass.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.700
- [Narrator] The
Republican-controlled House

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.260
and the Democrat-controlled Senate

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.560
will also have to negotiate a
full year spending agreement.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.830
- You know I'm not a fan
of government shutdowns.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:06.963
I've seen a few of them over the years.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.070
They never have produced a policy change,

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.310
and they've always been
a loser for Republicans.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.600
- [Narrator] Shut down or not,

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.210
the slim majority and political
infighting in the House

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.490
aren't going to go away for McCarthy.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.420
- There's still the looming question

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:22.980
of "If not McCarthy, then who?"

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.660
His number two, Steve Scalise,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.370
is undergoing cancer treatments.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.750
There's other Republicans

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.100
who could theoretically leave the caucus,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.720
but it's not clear that any of them

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:33.510
would be in a better position to do it.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.550
For McCarthy, it's just another example

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.680
of where he's in this crazy situation

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.010
and looking for a Houdini-like
trick to wiggle his way out,

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.332
and it's just not clear
how he's gonna do that.

00:04:43.332 --> 00:04:46.582
(graceful piano music)

